A1. This carries the light. ~ BEAM
A4. These are pounded to beat the band. ~ DRUMS
A8. What straws are good for. ~ SIPS
A12. A conjunction. ~ AND
A14. A second-hand car isn't much good for this. ~ USE
A16. This means before. ~ ERE
A17. The sun-god. ~ RA
A18. Many use this to get along in London. ~ TRAM
A20. A marital alibi. ~ TALE
A23. What they want in the movies. ~ IT
A24. This is sweet enough to eat. ~ ECLAIR
A27. Supposedly lugubrious individuals who laugh. (Tune in on any station.) ~ CLOWNS
A29. Garment. ~ TOG
A30. The way we'd feel crossing the Styx. ~ EERIE
A33. An unhatched insect. ~ NIT
A36. Boxers do this to each other. ~ MIT
A39. Dumb Dora thinks this is kept in an apiary. ~ MONKEY
A43. What the old-fashioned girl does to get the dirt. ~ SWEEPS
A46. When aviators go to find their place in the sun. ~ UP
A47. These get the raspberry. ~ ICES
A48. Scottish Celt. ~ GAEL
A49. Preposition expressing position. ~ AT
A50. There is a lot of love stuff in this game. ~ TENNIS
A53. This is a propeller. ~ PADDLE
A55. What baby wears to a dinner party. ~ BIB
A57. This often makes a hit. ~ BAT
A59. What horizontal 57 sometimes makes. ~ HOMER
A61. This word refers to an ancient empire. (Abbr.) ~ ROM
A63. This makes laws we cannot ignore with impunity. ~ NATURE
A65. Any one who does this will get all tied up. ~ ELOPES
A68. Conjunction. ~ OR
A69. What was Homer. ~ BARD
A71. It's unsafe for married men to carry this home late at night. ~ LOAD
A72. Baby's first word. ~ DA
A73. This is past for the poets. ~ OER
A75. This makes one all wrought up. ~ IRE
A77. A grain. ~ OAT
A78. Past tense of a verb which applies to informed people. ~ KNEW
A79. What David did when he fought Goliath. ~ SMOTE
A80. This has water all around it. ~ ISLE

D1. This kind of thing the reformers want to cover up. ~ BARE
D2. What law-makers do. ~ ENACT
D3. You can find this in the papers. ~ AD
D5. Captain Kidd's treasure. ~ RUM
D6. To whom Al Smith will appeal. ~ US
D7. What Tunney and Heeney did. ~ MET
D9. That is. ~ IE
D10. An invention of the Chinese. ~ PRINT
D11. What Helen Wills takes easily. ~ SETS
